1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:01,740
At one of our workshops, a team arrived

2
00:00:01,780 --> 00:00:03,640
with a clear proposal: we want

3
00:00:03,740 --> 00:00:05,440
artificial intelligence to tell us which

4
00:00:05,500 --> 00:00:06,720
requests to handle first.

5
00:00:07,260 --> 00:00:09,440
The demonstration was fast, tidy, and

6
00:00:09,540 --> 00:00:10,380
quite convincing.

7
00:00:11,220 --> 00:00:13,000
Before trying it, I asked a question:

8
00:00:13,420 --> 00:00:14,220
what does first mean?

9
00:00:14,780 --> 00:00:15,600
The most urgent?

10
00:00:16,020 --> 00:00:16,760
The easiest?

11
00:00:17,320 --> 00:00:18,800
The one that has been waiting longest?

12
00:00:19,600 --> 00:00:21,000
Within minutes, we had three different

13
00:00:21,080 --> 00:00:21,700
criteria.

14
00:00:22,120 --> 00:00:23,260
The tool could not resolve a

15
00:00:23,320 --> 00:00:24,360
disagreement we had not yet

16
00:00:24,420 --> 00:00:25,000
acknowledged.

17
00:00:25,720 --> 00:00:27,160
We worked with fictional cases and

18
00:00:27,240 --> 00:00:28,720
agreed on the decision we wanted to

19
00:00:28,760 --> 00:00:29,380
support.

20
00:00:29,980 --> 00:00:31,780
Then we examined the information we

21
00:00:31,820 --> 00:00:33,000
needed and what was missing.

22
00:00:33,640 --> 00:00:35,260
A short request is not always simple.

23
00:00:35,840 --> 00:00:37,380
Someone who writes more does not always

24
00:00:37,420 --> 00:00:38,080
need more help.

25
00:00:38,720 --> 00:00:40,340
Only then did we compare the system's

26
00:00:40,360 --> 00:00:42,140
suggestion with a review by someone who

27
00:00:42,200 --> 00:00:43,300
understood the service.

28
00:00:44,080 --> 00:00:45,600
When they disagreed, we did not

29
00:00:45,660 --> 00:00:47,440
automatically choose either answer.

30
00:00:48,140 --> 00:00:49,320
We looked for the reason behind the

31
00:00:49,360 --> 00:00:49,900
difference.

32
00:00:50,600 --> 00:00:52,140
That exercise taught us more than a

33
00:00:52,180 --> 00:00:53,400
screen full of results.

34
00:00:54,040 --> 00:00:56,340
It helped us uncover assumptions, refine

35
00:00:56,380 --> 00:00:58,760
our criteria, and decide when a person

36
00:00:58,800 --> 00:00:59,980
needed to review a case.

37
00:01:01,080 --> 00:01:02,500
Before your next experiment with

38
00:01:02,560 --> 00:01:03,960
artificial intelligence, write down

39
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:06,240
three things: which decision you want to

40
00:01:06,280 --> 00:01:08,200
improve, what information would be

41
00:01:08,240 --> 00:01:10,540
sufficient, and how you would recognise

42
00:01:10,600 --> 00:01:11,380
a wrong result.

43
00:01:12,220 --> 00:01:14,140
If you cannot explain those yet, start

44
00:01:14,180 --> 00:01:14,440
there.

45
00:01:15,180 --> 00:01:17,140
The speed of an answer can impress us.

46
00:01:17,760 --> 00:01:19,400
Its value lies in explaining why it

47
00:01:19,420 --> 00:01:22,160
deserves our trust, when it does not,

48
00:01:22,240 --> 00:01:24,000
and who takes responsibility for acting

49
00:01:24,040 --> 00:01:24,400
on it.
